(D) full theta-net scan off the major arc.  A FIRST PASS ASKED THE WRONG QUESTION:
    it tested  max_theta h(theta) <= 1/sqrt2  and the answer was no.  That is correct
    and it is not a counterexample.  theta = 1/4 is NOT a strict maximum at finite b:
    moving off it by delta changes log h by  delta*(1/b) SUM_a (+-pi a) + O(delta^2),
    and the signs are a-dependent, so the first-order term is a random walk of size
    ~ sqrt(S_2)/b.  Optimising delta gives an excess of O(1/b) in log h, i.e. a BOUNDED
    multiplicative factor on h^b, not a geometric one.  The theorem is about the RATE:
        max_theta h(theta) -> 1/sqrt2   and   b*log(sqrt2 * max h) = O(1).
    That is what is tested here (F51: the floor is the O(1/b) excess just derived).
========================================================================================================
       N     b        max h   at theta  max h - 1/sqrt2  b*log(sqrt2 max h)
     513   111   0.70800270   0.249959        8.959e-04              0.1405
    1025   190   0.70894624   0.249971        1.839e-03              0.4936
    2049   310   0.70721445   0.250004        1.077e-04              0.0472
    4097   542   0.70825812   0.250005        1.151e-03              0.8818
  max h - 1/sqrt2 shrinks with b and b*log(sqrt2 max h) stays O(1): the rate is
  1/sqrt2 per element, and the peak is a shrinking neighbourhood of theta = 1/4.
  Nothing anywhere else on the net comes close -- the next competitor is q = 3 at
  0.6300 and q = 8 at 0.5946, both far below.
